## 9. Three Versions of the Kernel Theorem

Following the programme's architecture, we separate the theorem into
three versions of increasing sophistication and decreasing generality.

### Version 1: Finite Combinatorial Theorem

Over a finite connected graph G with a ZMod n-valued connection ω,
for any k : ZMod n and a flat connection (no wrap edges), the constant
functions f(v) = c are in the kernel of the connection Laplacian L_k.

This is the correct finite combinatorial statement for the ZMod n
connection Laplacian as defined. The kernel dimension is at least
|π₀(G)| (one constant per connected component).

The programme's original spanning-tree potential claim is documented
as mathematically incorrect in `main_holonomy_annihilator_kernel`.
-/

/-- Version 1: Finite combinatorial kernel theorem.
  For a finite connected graph G with a flat connection ω (no wrap edges),
  for any k : ZMod n, every constant function is in the kernel of L_k.

  This gives a lower bound dim(ker L_k) ≥ |π₀(G)| on the kernel dimension.
  The proof is `main_holonomy_annihilator_kernel` (flat-connection case). -/
theorem kernel_theorem_v1 (k : ZMod n) (c : ZMod n)
    (hconn : Fintype.card G.graph.ConnectedComponent = 1)
    (h_flat : ∀ e : Sym2 G.V, ¬ G.wrap e) :
    (Matrix.toLin' (laplacianOnCharacter ω k)) (extendToBlock ω (fun _ => c)) = 0 := by
  exact main_holonomy_annihilator_kernel ω k c h_flat

